{
  "heading": "ARIA states from true-or-false fields",
  "target": "web",
  "body": "Bind a true-or-false field to an `aria-*` attribute, and the page writes the text `true` or `false`. A screen reader reads that text as the state: `aria-expanded=\"false\"` says the menu is closed. This works the same way in every Tac service language, because the shared page runtime writes the attribute. A Go field `Expanded bool`, a Rust field `expanded: bool` and a Python field `expanded: bool` all give the same `aria-expanded` value.\n\nOther attributes keep the HTML rule: `true` writes the attribute with an empty value, and `false` removes it, so `:hidden=\"!menu.expanded\"` hides the list while the menu is closed. A JavaScript `null` value removes any attribute, `aria-*` included. Text keeps its value: `:aria-current=\"menu.current\"` writes `page` when the field holds `page`.",
  "axis": "language",
